items: tolong-on-a-string-answers-four-different-things
paths: file:v2/src/Runtime.scala file:v2/backend/js/JsBackend.scala file:v2/backend/rust/RustBackend.scala file:v2/conformance/tolong-on-a-string.coreir file:tests/e2e/rust-toint-parity-gate.sh file:v2/backend/jvm/JvmBackend.scala
scope: |
  `"8".toLong` answers FOUR different things, on a method `specs/numeric-widths.md` §2.1 declares a
  synonym of `toInt` ("Int and Long are the same runtime type, not merely the same width"):

    run-ir  refuses, "a method that does not exist survived as <closure>"
    jvm     8
    js      throws "no dispatch for .toLong"
    rust    0, silently

  The root is the VM: there is no `(StrV, "toLong")` arm, and the `<closure>` refusal is the
  downstream symptom rather than a decision. Its message is loud but misleading — it says the method
  does not exist, when what did not exist was the arm.

  WIDENED to `v2/backend/jvm/JvmBackend.scala` BEFORE the edit, on a measurement rather than a
  hunch: asking the same question of junk input found the JVM generator answers `"abc".toInt` = **0**
  and keeps running, where the VM aborts. Same silent zero as the Rust one fixed yesterday, third
  generator with it, and not in any entry. JS was measured too and needs nothing there — it throws.

  NARROW otherwise: the String arms only. The Option forms go to the VM and JS because that is where
  `toIntOption` already lives; adding them to the two generators that have never had it would be a
  different task.
next: VM arm + js synonym + rust arm + jvm junk behaviour, then a conformance fixture and the gate
